# Spectro-Temporal Signal Analysis of Doppler Twinkling Artifacts

## Overview

This project investigates the spectro-temporal characteristics of color Doppler twinkling in polymethyl methacrylate (PMMA) markers and compares them with non-twinkling metal targets.

## Motivation

Color Doppler twinkling can improve ultrasound visualization of highly reflective targets, but the signal characteristics responsible for the artifact remain unclear. This project examines RF and IQ signals to identify temporal and frequency-domain features associated with twinkling.

## Methods

- Verasonics V1 ultrasound system
- PMMA twinkling marker and non-twinkling metal target
- Plane-wave Doppler transmission
- 4 MHz transmit frequency
- 2.5 kHz pulse repetition frequency
- Delay-and-sum beamforming
- IQ demodulation
- Wall filtering
- Pulse-wise and frame-wise signal analysis
- RF and IQ frequency-spectrum analysis

## Signal-Processing Pipeline

![Signal-processing framework](Picture7.svg)

Raw channel RF data were processed through beamforming, IQ demodulation, wall filtering, and Doppler estimation.

## Temporal Analysis

![Beamformed RF signal analysis](Picture8.svg)

PMMA showed greater temporal variation than metal. The metal response remained relatively stable, whereas the twinkling signal showed greater changes across repeated pulses and frames.

## Frequency-Domain Analysis

![RF frequency-spectrum analysis](Picture9.svg)

Beamformed RF signals were analyzed in the frequency domain before and after wall filtering. PMMA showed broader and more irregular spectral content, whereas metal produced a smoother and more stable spectral response.

## IQ Spectral Analysis

![IQ frequency-spectrum analysis](Picture4.svg)

IQ analysis showed a similar difference between the two materials. PMMA showed greater spectral variation, whereas metal maintained a more stable frequency response.

## Key Findings

- PMMA showed greater temporal signal variability than metal.
- Wall filtering revealed differences between twinkling and stable reflector signals.
- PMMA showed broader and more irregular RF spectral content.
- Spectral differences remained after normalization.
- IQ analysis showed similar spectral differences after demodulation.
- Temporal and frequency-domain features provide complementary measures of the twinkling response.

